WEBVTT

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.880
Serving an LLM over an API means making model inference available to applications through

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.000
a controlled request and response interface.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Instead of a person typing into a local chat window,

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.000
application code sends structured requests to a service and receives model output back.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.091
That service may support chat completions, text completions, embeddings,

00:00:17.091 --> 00:00:20.000
structured output, streaming responses, or a mix of these patterns.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.990
LLM serving can be local, private, cloud-based, or hybrid.

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:25.980
A local service might run on a workstation or homelab server.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.955
A private service might run inside an organization-controlled environment.

00:00:29.955 --> 00:00:32.990
A cloud service may provide managed scaling and model access.

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:36.955
A hybrid design might route some requests to local models and others to hosted providers.

00:00:36.955 --> 00:00:41.030
Each pattern has different implications for privacy, latency, cost, reliability,

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:42.210
and operations.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:46.250
Running a model interactively is not the same as operating it as a service.

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:51.215
Interactive use is often forgiving: one user, one prompt, visible errors,

00:00:51.215 --> 00:00:53.010
and manual retries.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:55.760
An API service has callers, credentials, request limits, logs, monitoring, failure modes,

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:56.960
and user expectations.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.650
It has to handle malformed input, concurrent requests, timeouts, model changes,

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.000
and downstream systems that may rely on its output.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.770
API serving requires attention to performance, access control, privacy, reliability,

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:09.000
and observability.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.000
The goal is not merely to get a model responding.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.955
The goal is to provide inference through boundaries that applications can use safely:

00:01:14.955 --> 00:01:18.955
callers are authenticated, access is scoped, data handling is understood,

00:01:18.955 --> 00:01:20.120
output is monitored,

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:23.777
and operational behavior is predictable enough to support real workflows.
